  "name": "Fischer-Tropsch Synthetic Fuel (short queries)",
  "script": "During World War II, Germany faced a severe oil shortage because it had very little natural petroleum. To keep its tanks, aircraft, and industries running, German scientists converted abundant coal into synthetic fuel using the Fischer–Tropsch process. Decades later, isolated South Africa adopted the same technology to survive international oil embargoes during apartheid, leading to the rise of Sasol. This remarkable process proved that a nation could transform coal into liquid fuel when crude oil was unavailable. Today, as the world searches for cleaner energy, coal-to-liquid technology remains one of history's most fascinating engineering achievements.",
  "language": "en",
